import meta::pure::test::pct::*;

'''
Whether a regular expression matches a string in full.

**The pattern must match the entire string**, not merely occur somewhere in it, so `'abc'` does not
match `'[a-z][a-z][a-z][a-z]'` and would not match a bare `'a'` either. Reach for
`regexpLike(String[1], String[1]):Boolean[1]` when a containment search is what you want; it also
takes matching options, which this does not.

The whole pattern is anchored, not its first alternative: `'cat|dog'` matches `'cat'` and `'dog'`
but neither `'catfish'` nor `'hotdog'`. Equivalently, `matches(s, p)` is
`regexpLike(s, '^(?:' + p + ')$')`.

Patterns use `java.util.regex` (Java regular expression) syntax. Not every construct survives
pushdown to a relational store — see `docs/engineering/reference/regex-portability.md` for what is
portable across execution targets.

**Parameters**
- `string` — the string to test.
- `regexp` — the pattern, which must match the whole string.

**Returns** `true` when the pattern matches the entire string.

**Examples**
```pure
'hello'->matches('hello')                 // true
'abc'->matches('[a-z][a-z][a-z]')         // true
'abc'->matches('[a-z][a-z][a-z][a-z]')    // false, the pattern must cover the whole string
'catfish'->matches('cat|dog')             // false, the anchoring spans the alternation
```

**See also** `regexpLike(String[1], String[1]):Boolean[1]`
'''
native function <<PCT.function>>
  meta::pure::functions::string::matches(string:String[1], regexp:String[1]):Boolean[1];

function <<PCT.test>> meta::pure::functions::string::tests::matches::testMatches<Z|y>(f:Function<{Function<{->Z[y]}>[1]->Z[y]}>[1]):Boolean[1]
{
  assert($f->eval(|'hello'->matches('hello')));
  assert($f->eval(|'abc'->matches('[a-z][a-z][a-z]')));
}

function <<PCT.test>> meta::pure::functions::string::tests::matches::testMatchesNoMatch<Z|y>(f:Function<{Function<{->Z[y]}>[1]->Z[y]}>[1]):Boolean[1]
{
  assertFalse($f->eval(|'abc'->matches('[a-z][a-z][a-z][a-z]')));
}

'''
Anchoring covers the whole pattern, including a top-level alternation.

A store that builds the anchored form by concatenation produces `'^cat|dog$'`, which every SQL regex
engine reads as `(^cat)|(dog$)` — matching `'catfish'` and `'hotdog'`. The anchors have to group the
alternation for the result to agree with the platform.
'''
function <<PCT.test>> meta::pure::functions::string::tests::matches::testMatchesAlternation<Z|y>(f:Function<{Function<{->Z[y]}>[1]->Z[y]}>[1]):Boolean[1]
{
  assert($f->eval(|'cat'->matches('cat|dog')));
  assert($f->eval(|'dog'->matches('cat|dog')));
  assertFalse($f->eval(|'catfish'->matches('cat|dog')));
  assertFalse($f->eval(|'hotdog'->matches('cat|dog')));
}

'''
An unanchored pattern still has to cover the whole string.

Separates `matches` from `regexpLike` on a store that implements the former with a containment
operator: `'abcd'` contains `'bc'`, so a containment answer would be `true` here.
'''
function <<PCT.test>> meta::pure::functions::string::tests::matches::testMatchesUnanchoredIsFullMatch<Z|y>(f:Function<{Function<{->Z[y]}>[1]->Z[y]}>[1]):Boolean[1]
{
  assertFalse($f->eval(|'abcd'->matches('bc')));
  assert($f->eval(|'abcd'->matches('a.*d')));
}
